            <ArticleTitle>Quality of life impact related to foot health in a sample of sea workers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>146-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jtv.2015.07.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0965-206X(15)00069-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">People working at sea show a marked presence of musculoskeletal pain as well as an important coexistence of pain in diverse anatomical regions, which is believed to have a harmful effect on the quality of life related to foot health. The aim is to describe and compare the impact in a sample of sea workers and people who work on the ground in the light of the scores obtained with regard foot health and health in general.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A sample of 94 participants of a mean age of 41.29 ± 10.603 came to a health centre where self-reported data were registered, informants' professional activity was determined and the scores obtained were compared in the Foot Health Status Questionnaire.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The sea workers group showed a worse quality of life related to health in general and to foot health specifically. Differences between the two groups were evaluated by means of a t-test for independent samples, showing statistical significance (P &lt; 0.05) for the dimensions of pain and general health related to the foot.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">People working at sea present a negative impact on the quality of life related to foot health, which appears to be associated with the presence of deformities, sprains, plantar warts and fungus.</AbstractText>
